ce593e685c3c84ca8adea7c09808b4d7605f13e8	<p>O WERE my Love yon lilac fair,<br/>\n    Wi' purple blossoms to the spring,<br/>\nAnd I a bird to shelter there,<br/>\n    When wearied on my little wing;<br/>\nHow I wad mourn when it was torn<br/>\n    By autumn wild and winter rude!<br/>\nBut I wad sing on wanton wing<br/>\n    When youthfu' May its bloom renew'd.</p><p>O gin my Love were yon red rose<br/>\n    That grows upon the castle wa',<br/>\nAnd I mysel a drap o' dew,<br/>\n    Into her bonnie breast to fa';<br/>\nO there, beyond expression blest,<br/>\n    I'd feast on beauty a' the night;<br/>\nSeal'd on her silk-saft faulds to rest,<br/>\n    Till fley'd awa' by Phoebus' light.</p>	I wish my love was a lilac or a red rose, to be with her forever.	O WERE my Love yon lilac fair,\n    Wi' purple blossoms to the spring,\nAnd I a bird to shelter there,\n    When wearied on my little wing;\nHow I wad mourn when it was torn\n    By autumn wild and winter rude!\nBut I wad sing on wanton wing\n    When youthfu' May its bloom renew'd.O gin my Love were yon red rose\n    That grows upon the castle wa',\nAnd I mysel a drap o' dew,\n    Into her bonnie breast to fa';\nO there, beyond expression blest,\n    I'd feast on beauty a' the night;\nSeal'd on her silk-saft faulds to rest,\n    Till fley'd awa' by Phoebus' light.	Robert Burns	https://englishverse.com/poets/burns_robert/../../poems/o_were_my_love_yon_lilac_fair	0	2023-11-20 20:43:34.637512
e88009dce1ea1825cd7f16e63f7383251e737f44	<p>On Salathiel Pavy<br/>\nA child of Queen Elizabeth's Chapel</p><p>WEEP with me, all you that read<br/>\n        This little story;<br/>\nAnd know, for whom a tear you shed<br/>\n        Death's self is sorry.<br/>\n'Twas a child that so did thrive<br/>\n        In grace and feature,<br/>\nAs Heaven and Nature seem'd to strive<br/>\n        Which own'd the creature.<br/>\nYears he number'd scarce thirteen<br/>\n        When Fates turn'd cruel,<br/>\nYet three fill'd zodiacs had he been<br/>\n        The stage's jewel;<br/>\nAnd did act (what now we moan)<br/>\n        Old men so duly,<br/>\nAs sooth the Parcae thought him one,<br/>\n        He play'd so truly.<br/>\nSo, by error, to his fate<br/>\n        They all consented;<br/>\nBut, viewing him since, alas, too late!<br/>\n        They have repented;<br/>\nAnd have sought, to give new birth,<br/>\n        In baths to steep him;<br/>\nBut, being so much too good for earth,<br/>\n        Heaven vows to keep him.</p>	Mourn the death of a talented child actor.	On Salathiel Pavy\nA child of Queen Elizabeth's ChapelWEEP with me, all you that read\n        This little story;\nAnd know, for whom a tear you shed\n        Death's self is sorry.\n'Twas a child that so did thrive\n        In grace and feature,\nAs Heaven and Nature seem'd to strive\n        Which own'd the creature.\nYears he number'd scarce thirteen\n        When Fates turn'd cruel,\nYet three fill'd zodiacs had he been\n        The stage's jewel;\nAnd did act (what now we moan)\n        Old men so duly,\nAs sooth the Parcae thought him one,\n        He play'd so truly.\nSo, by error, to his fate\n        They all consented;\nBut, viewing him since, alas, too late!\n        They have repented;\nAnd have sought, to give new birth,\n        In baths to steep him;\nBut, being so much too good for earth,\n        Heaven vows to keep him.	Ben Jonson	https://englishverse.com/poets/jonson_ben/../../poems/epitaph_ii_on_sal